.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1053 PM PDT Fri Oct 16 2026
(Tonight through Sunday)

Clear skies continue this evening, but the offshore winds have
calmed substantially. The SFO-WMC gradient peaked at -13.7 mb
Friday morning and has since weakened to -7.8 mb. 24 hours ago Mt.
St. Helena was reporting NNE winds of 44 mph. Now it`s NW at 5.
The 500 mb ridge over northern California is weakening. The 00Z
balloon measured the 500 mb height at 5910m, down from 5930m at
12Z. That`s still above the 90th percentile for this time of year,
but the trend will accelerate through the day Saturday as the
Channel Islands cut-off low gets absorbed back into the jet
stream, bringing more zonal flow to the upper levels.

At the surface this means the return of onshore winds and the
start of a cooling trend. Max temperatures Saturday are expected
to be a couple degrees cooler than Friday. It will still be a
warm, sunny day for everyone. The bigger change is Sunday. A new
deep low pressure system approaches from the west. This system
will bring back the marine layer and drop the max temperatures by
another 10-15 degrees. It will also bring another round of wind,
but from the west this time. Our current forecast shows gusts up
to 50 mph through Altamont Pass Sunday night. That`s the outlier.
Most places will have gusts in the 15-25 mph range.

