.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1053 PM PDT Fri Oct 16 2026
(Sunday night through next Friday)

A brief period of zonal flow aloft gets interrupted on Monday as an
upper-level trough pushes southward along the California coast.
Ahead of the low pressure system, onshore winds increase across the
Bay Area and Central Coast with a deepening marine layer, resulting
in cooling to near seasonal temperatures. The trough becomes a cut-
off low by Tuesday and lingers off the Central Californian coast
before gradually propagating inland through Southern California
Wednesday evening into Thursday. The system`s position allows for
the reinforcement of cold air advection across the Bay Area and
Monterey Bay regions through the end of next week.

In terms of precipitation chances, light rain showers are possible
Tuesday through Thursday across the Bay Area and Monterey Bay
regions. The coastline and southernmost extent of the Central Coast
have a 40-60% probability of light rain meanwhile the North, East,
and South Bay have a 20-40% chance. In addition to rain, there is
potential for thunderstorm development during the aforementioned
time period. The three main ingredients required for thunderstorm
development are instability, lift, and moisture. Given the complex
terrain within the region, we can guarantee some degree of
orographic lifting as the system moves inland. Both the European and
GFS ensemble members are hinting at precipitable water values around
the 75th percentile for this time of year, so we can also check
moisture off our thunderstorm ingredient list. Finally, we have
instability. European ensemble members are exhibiting 500 mb
temperatures around -15 to -20 degrees Celsius, which falls below
the 10th percentile for this time of year. Meanwhile, the 850 mb
temperatures are around the seasonal normal of 9 to 12 degrees
Celsius. Atmospheric instability may arise from the mixing of air
masses, particularly when a cold air mass sits on top of a warm air
mass. Since cold air is more dense than warm air, it will sink to
the surface while forcing the ascent of warm air. As warm air rises,
it cools adiabatically and condenses, resulting in latent heat
release, which further warms the surrounding air allowing for
increased vertical mixing. This process is dependent on other
thermodynamic factors, however the structure of our cut-off low
supports an unstable system due to vertical mixing.

Despite all of the above, there is still high uncertainty regarding
the rainfall and thunderstorm potential as it is dependent on the
exact trajectory of the cut-off low and the alignment of various
elements. Be sure to check back on the forecast for updated
information.

